            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a moderate amount of air leakage. 



Because your house is under-ventilated, I do NOT recommend doing any air sealing work until you have installed mechanical ventilation such as an HRV. See the section of this report on ventilation for further details. 



I noted a significant amount of air leakage through the attic hatch. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



As you have not finished installing the trim around your new windows, I recommend you make sure that these locations are well insulated and sealed before installing the trim around the windows. 



I did not note any air leakage around the frames of your exterior doors, however I do recommend that you keep the weather stripping well maintained. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



I also recommend that you perform some air sealing around the header in your basement before replacing any of the insulation in that space. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $70  per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ, and qualify for up to$500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



When you add the exterior insulation, I recommend that you continue the exterior insulation down over the header, so that this area is also insulated. 



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have R28 of fiberglass batt insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



As I noted during my site assessment, the insulation in your attic is not evenly distributed. There is a lot of insulation piled up near the attic hatch, and less insulation in other areas. 



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $125 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 2x6 framed walls with R20 insulation and no concrete structure. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your basement.



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially save $150 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $625 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for basement insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a basement insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program







</foundation>
                <windows>

As you have already replaced all your upstairs windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the four basement windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the four basement windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $40 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.2 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

Modelling showed that currently, your home is underventilated. This means that in order to maintain a healthy indoor environment, you will need to install mechanical ventilation before performing any air sealing work in your home



Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV could be tied into the existing furnace duct work to minimize installation requirements.



While the addition of an HRV will increase the costs to operate your home, it will help ensure that you have a safe and comfortable indoor environment, and help protect the air quality inside your home. 



I also strongly recommend that you change the dryer vent in your basement so that it is venting outside. With it's current set up, it is venting into the basement. This means that any moisture that is removed from your clothes will stay in your basement, and this could potentially create air quality problems. 



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, you could potentially spend an additional $110 per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.865 per litre

Wood - $315.00 per cord 

Pellets - $7.35 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@gmail.com



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
